        <div id="folding-vs-rolling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Folding vs Rolling Miter Saw Stands
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first decision is whether you carry the stand or roll it. That single choice sets the price, the weight and how quickly you can start cutting, and it depends almost entirely on how often the saw moves.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Folding miter saw stands</h3>

          <p>
            A folding stand like the DeWalt DWX724, Makita WST06, Milwaukee 48-08-0551 or Ryobi QuickStand is a beam on folding legs. You carry it out, unfold it, then lift the saw onto its brackets. They weigh around 30 to 35 pounds, fold to about 44 inches long, and cost the least. The extra step of lifting the saw on and off is the catch: a 12 inch sliding saw can weigh 50 to 65 pounds, and carrying that separately is the part most people dislike.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rolling miter saw stands</h3>

          <p>
            A rolling stand like the Bosch T4B or DeWalt DWX726 keeps the saw bolted on permanently and folds up on its own wheels. You roll the whole thing like a hand truck, flip it up, and cut. Setup takes seconds and you never lift the saw by itself. The cost is weight: the T4B alone is about 77 pounds before the saw, and lifting the combination into a truck bed is a two-person or ramp job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our rule of thumb is simple. If the saw lives in one place and moves out to the driveway a few times a month, a folding stand or a DIY station is better value. If you set up on a jobsite more than once or twice a week, or you work alone and hate lifting the saw, a rolling stand pays for itself in time and backs. The Bosch GTA3800 sits between the two: a folding stand with wheels on the frame, but the saw still comes off for transport.
          </p>

