        <div id="support-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Support Length and Load Capacity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Support length is the spec that actually changes your cuts. It is the total span from the end of one extended support arm to the other, and it tells you how long a board the stand can hold level without help. Most compact stands land between 8 and 10 feet. The Bosch GTA3800 reaches about 12.5 feet, and the T4B quotes up to 16 feet with its rails fully out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about what you cut most often. Baseboard and door casing usually come in 8 to 16 foot lengths, but you rarely cut them full length; most cuts are near one end, and the other end hangs off the support. A 10 foot stand handles that easily. Crown molding, siding and long deck boards are where support length really matters, because a long board that sags on the outfeed side pinches the blade and throws off the angle. If you do a lot of that work, buy the longer stand or add a roller stand at the far end.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Load capacity is mostly a proxy</h3>

          <p>
            Ratings of 300 to 500 pounds look important, but no miter saw and board come close. A heavy 12 inch sliding saw is around 60 pounds, and even with a long hardwood board and someone leaning on it, you are well under 150 pounds. Treat the capacity rating as a hint about how stiff the beam and legs are, not as a real-world limit. A 500 pound stand flexes less than a 300 pound one under the same saw, and stiffness is what keeps your cuts square.
          </p>

          <p>
            What matters more is how the supports adjust. Each arm needs to lock at exactly the height of the saw table, and it needs to stay there when you push a heavy board onto it. Look for supports with a positive height lock rather than a friction knob that creeps, and a roller or flat pad that the board slides across without catching. Material stops that flip up on the supports are worth having too, because they make repeat cuts faster and more consistent than measuring each piece.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Specs by Type</p>
