        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best miter saw stand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the DeWalt DWX724 is the best miter saw stand: rated for 500 pounds, supports material up to 10 feet and weighs about 30 pounds. For daily jobsite use, the Bosch T4B Gravity-Rise is the best rolling stand. For long crown and trim, the Bosch GTA3800 reaches about 12.5 feet. On a budget, the Ryobi QuickStand handles weekend projects well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do miter saw stands fit all saws?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most do. Stands from DeWalt, Bosch, Makita, Milwaukee and Ryobi use universal brackets that fit most saws from major brands. A few compact or unusual saws need an adapter plate. Measure the distance between your saw's mounting holes and compare it with the bracket range listed in the stand's manual before you buy, to avoid a return.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a rolling or folding miter saw stand better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how often you move the saw. A rolling stand keeps the saw attached and sets up in seconds, which is worth it if you move it several times a week. A folding stand is lighter, cheaper and more compact, which suits homeowners and anyone whose saw lives mostly in one place.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much support length do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For baseboard, casing and most home projects, 8 to 10 feet of support is enough. For long crown molding, siding and long deck boards, look for 12 feet or more, such as the Bosch GTA3800 or T4B. You can always extend a shorter stand with a separate roller stand set to the same height.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I build a DIY miter saw station instead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your saw stays in one shop, a DIY station is often better. It gives you long, flush support on both sides, storage underneath and dust collection, and it costs less than a premium rolling stand. The trade-off is that it does not travel, so many people keep a folding stand for jobs away from the shop.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What height should a miter saw stand be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most stands put the saw table around 32 to 36 inches high, similar to a kitchen counter, which is comfortable for most people. Taller users may prefer a stand at the upper end of that range. For a DIY station, build the top around 36 inches and recess the saw so its table sits flush with the wings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a miter saw on a workbench instead of a stand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the bench is sturdy and long enough. The problem is support: a long board hanging off one side of a bench sags and can pinch the blade. Add blocks or roller stands at the same height as the saw table, or build a recessed DIY station so the bench top lines up with the saw.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
